1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,000
A battle against the ferocious

2
00:00:03,100 --> 00:00:04,900
Atlantic.

3
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,440
Safe passage through treacherous waters, an engineering feat

4
00:00:08,540 --> 00:00:10,740
that many believed impossible.

5
00:00:10,840 --> 00:00:13,940
Now, the Cape Cod Canal on Modern Marvelous.

6
00:00:13,941 --> 00:00:15,940
of the mountains.

7
00:00:29,060 --> 00:00:33,460
Cape Cod is a 70-mile-long finger of land steeped in history

8
00:00:33,560 --> 00:00:36,460
and romance.

9
00:00:36,560 --> 00:00:41,860
But the waters around the Cape are home to violent storms,

10
00:00:41,861 --> 00:00:45,060
Dense falls and shifting sands.

11
00:00:45,061 --> 00:00:47,760
For hundreds of years, they were a mariner's nightmare.

12
00:00:51,760 --> 00:00:54,400
Throughout America's short maritime history,

13
00:00:54,401 --> 00:00:57,300
thousands of ships sank, foundered,

14
00:00:57,301 --> 00:01:00,520
or were destroyed on the shoals and rocky shores of Cape Cod.

15
00:01:03,920 --> 00:01:08,160
But today, ships no longer have to brave these treacherous waters,

16
00:01:08,161 --> 00:01:10,860
thanks to the world's widest sea level canal,

17
00:01:10,861 --> 00:01:18,400
spanning 480 feet from shore to shore, the Cape Cod Canal.

18
00:01:18,401 --> 00:01:21,700
There are no locks in the 17.4 mile canal,

19
00:01:21,701 --> 00:01:24,800
but there are natural obstacles that have at times proven

20
00:01:24,801 --> 00:01:27,800
disastrous.

21
00:01:27,801 --> 00:01:31,020
But without the canal, maritime traffic would be tremendously

22
00:01:31,021 --> 00:01:32,320
altered.

23
00:01:32,321 --> 00:01:36,620
The canal stays on the order of 150 miles, 130 miles,

24
00:01:36,621 --> 00:01:39,420
depending on which track a vessel would take.

25
00:01:39,421 --> 00:01:41,080
But more important than the mileage, I think,

26
00:01:41,180 --> 00:01:43,920
is the safety of the canal.

27
00:01:44,020 --> 00:01:45,760
The canal wasn't a luxury.

28
00:01:45,860 --> 00:01:48,260
It was a necessity.

29
00:01:48,360 --> 00:01:50,300
Built for two-way traffic, the canal

30
00:01:50,400 --> 00:01:53,740
is composed of a land cut that is seven miles long,

31
00:01:53,840 --> 00:01:56,040
with open water channels on either end.

32
00:01:56,140 --> 00:01:58,340
One channel extends into Buzzards Bay,

33
00:01:58,440 --> 00:02:02,300
and the other into Cape Cod Bay.

34
00:02:02,400 --> 00:02:06,180
Operated and maintained by the US Army Corps of Engineers,

35
00:02:06,280 --> 00:02:08,840
the Cape Cod Canal uses a state-of-the-art marine

36
00:02:08,841 --> 00:02:12,840
control system, monitoring an average of 20,000 vessels each

37
00:02:12,841 --> 00:02:15,840
year. Commercial and private vessels up

38
00:02:15,841 --> 00:02:22,840
to 825 feet long use the canal 24 hours a day, seven days a week.

39
00:02:22,841 --> 00:02:26,840
Any vessel longer than 825 feet would have extreme difficulty

40
00:02:26,841 --> 00:02:30,840
maneuvering inside the two-way traffic land cut and isn't

41
00:02:30,841 --> 00:02:34,840
allowed inside the canal. Since the canal essentially made

42
00:02:34,841 --> 00:02:38,840
made Cape Cod an island. Three bridges were built to span

43
00:02:38,841 --> 00:02:43,340
the canal and link Cape Cod to the rest of Massachusetts.

44
00:02:43,341 --> 00:02:46,280
Two highway bridges, the Sagamore Bridge and Bourne

45
00:02:46,281 --> 00:02:51,900
Bridge, and the Buzzards Bay Railroad Bridge.

46
00:02:51,901 --> 00:02:55,400
More than 36 million vehicles pass over the Sagamore and Bourne

47
00:02:55,401 --> 00:02:59,900
Bridges every year. The Buzzards Bay Railroad

48
00:02:59,901 --> 00:03:02,900
Bridge was the longest vertical lift bridge in the world at the

49
00:03:02,901 --> 00:03:09,540
at the time of its construction. Now used mostly for hauling refuse

50
00:03:09,541 --> 00:03:13,540
off the Cape, the Railroad and Buzzards Bay Railroad Bridge are

51
00:03:13,541 --> 00:03:18,540
still vital to Cape Cod's infrastructure.

52
00:03:18,541 --> 00:03:21,460
Although fewer than five operators man the Buzzards Bay Railroad

53
00:03:21,461 --> 00:03:25,860
Bridge, an average of 46 Army Corps of Engineers employees work

54
00:03:25,861 --> 00:03:31,060
on the canal. From marine traffic control to

55
00:03:31,061 --> 00:03:34,560
to bridge maintenance, to educational services

56
00:03:34,660 --> 00:03:36,900
for the community, the Army Corps of Engineers

57
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:40,040
has had stewardship of the canal for over three quarters

58
00:03:40,140 --> 00:03:41,200
of a century.

59
00:03:41,300 --> 00:03:43,940
We get all kinds of shipping traffic.

60
00:03:44,040 --> 00:03:46,280
We have a large number of tugs and barges

61
00:03:46,380 --> 00:03:49,220
that come through the canal, as well as passenger vessels,

62
00:03:49,320 --> 00:03:53,180
bulk carrying ships, ships that might carry coal or cement,

63
00:03:53,280 --> 00:03:55,020
tankers, of course.

64
00:03:55,120 --> 00:03:57,160
The largest product that comes through the canal

65
00:03:57,260 --> 00:04:01,060
are oil, petrochemical products.

66
00:04:01,061 --> 00:04:04,760
In the Marine Traffic Control Center located in Buzzards Bay,

67
00:04:04,860 --> 00:04:08,040
all commercial vessels and private vessels 65 feet or

68
00:04:08,140 --> 00:04:09,540
larger are tracked and monitored.

69
00:04:12,600 --> 00:04:15,740
There are safety issues in the canal, the current in the

70
00:04:15,840 --> 00:04:18,280
canal being the primary concern.

71
00:04:18,380 --> 00:04:22,080
Current can run as much as 5.2 knots, and it changes direction

72
00:04:22,180 --> 00:04:24,720
every six hours with a tide.

73
00:04:24,820 --> 00:04:27,980
The ocean's tides, the cyclical changes in sea level

74
00:04:27,981 --> 00:04:31,680
caused by the sun's and moon's gravitational pulls

75
00:04:31,780 --> 00:04:35,020
have a profound influence on the canal.

76
00:04:35,120 --> 00:04:37,020
The current in the canal is caused

77
00:04:37,120 --> 00:04:40,820
by two different tide cycles, one north of the Cape

78
00:04:40,920 --> 00:04:42,620
and the other south of the Cape.

79
00:04:42,720 --> 00:04:45,600
The rise and fall of the tide south of the Cape

80
00:04:45,700 --> 00:04:49,460
is only four feet. And the rise and fall of the tide

81
00:04:49,560 --> 00:04:52,800
north of the Cape is nine feet.

82
00:04:52,900 --> 00:04:54,680
The five-foot difference in tide levels

83
00:04:54,780 --> 00:04:57,980
on opposite ends of the canal generates a swift current

84
00:04:57,981 --> 00:05:00,620
as the rising water flows downhill.

85
00:05:00,720 --> 00:05:02,460
The current then reverses direction

86
00:05:02,560 --> 00:05:04,520
as the Cape's tide cycle continues.

87
00:05:08,460 --> 00:05:10,520
Sophisticated hardware and software

88
00:05:10,620 --> 00:05:14,460
monitor vessel movement and environmental conditions.

89
00:05:14,560 --> 00:05:18,960
This is environmental data at the five reporting stations,

90
00:05:19,060 --> 00:05:21,840
namely the wind speed, the wind direction,

91
00:05:21,940 --> 00:05:25,780
and the state of the tide, which is critical for the deep draft

92
00:05:25,880 --> 00:05:27,980
vessels who use our canal.

93
00:05:27,981 --> 00:05:31,420
We monitor the tides 24 hours a day in order to allow deep

94
00:05:31,421 --> 00:05:34,380
draft vessels to go through.

95
00:05:34,381 --> 00:05:36,580
The critical function of the Marine Traffic Control

96
00:05:36,581 --> 00:05:40,220
Station is to maintain a safe waterway for both the deep

97
00:05:40,221 --> 00:05:42,980
draft commercial vessels and the private recreational

98
00:05:42,981 --> 00:05:45,180
vessels.

99
00:05:45,181 --> 00:05:48,420
As you can see, we're tracking two westbound vessels and one

100
00:05:48,421 --> 00:05:51,540
eastbound vessel, tracking number 213.

101
00:05:51,541 --> 00:05:54,140
His course is 270 degrees true.

102
00:05:54,141 --> 00:05:55,740
He's going west.

103
00:05:55,741 --> 00:05:57,740
And his speed is seven knots.

104
00:05:57,741 --> 00:06:01,640
on the westbounder, and we'll find that the vessels will meet

105
00:06:01,740 --> 00:06:03,380
in 48 minutes.

106
00:06:03,480 --> 00:06:07,280
They'll meet between the two highway bridges.

107
00:06:07,380 --> 00:06:09,280
Deep draft vessels can't meet each other

108
00:06:09,380 --> 00:06:11,680
under any of the bridges.

109
00:06:11,780 --> 00:06:14,900
Marine traffic control must calculate the vessel's speeds

110
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:17,600
and communicate where on the canal the vessels can meet.

111
00:06:17,700 --> 00:06:19,700
Canal control on 14.

112
00:06:19,800 --> 00:06:22,100
On the canal itself, Army Corps patrol

113
00:06:22,200 --> 00:06:26,040
boats police the waterway, ready to assist any vessel

114
00:06:26,041 --> 00:06:29,080
and maintain a safe marine environment.

115
00:06:29,180 --> 00:06:35,280
This boat has two 435 horsepower Caterpillar diesels.

116
00:06:35,380 --> 00:06:39,820
And the other two patrol boats have two 430 horsepower

117
00:06:39,920 --> 00:06:40,760
Cummins diesels.

118
00:06:40,860 --> 00:06:43,660
It's pretty significant power.

119
00:06:43,760 --> 00:06:44,660
This is a 40 footer.

120
00:06:44,760 --> 00:06:46,300
The other two are 41s.

121
00:06:46,400 --> 00:06:48,760
These boats, every now and then,

122
00:06:48,860 --> 00:06:52,660
have kept tugging barges from hitting the riprap

123
00:06:52,760 --> 00:06:55,400
or turned a tug and barge around in here.

124
00:06:55,401 --> 00:06:59,400
So they have pretty good horsepower for a boat their size.

125
00:07:04,400 --> 00:07:07,940
Anything that loses power or becomes disabled in the canal,

126
00:07:08,040 --> 00:07:09,240
we consider an emergency.

127
00:07:09,340 --> 00:07:11,340
We get there as quick as we can.

128
00:07:11,440 --> 00:07:13,860
There could be a large ship coming around the bend.

129
00:07:13,960 --> 00:07:16,260
There could be a tug and barge coming around the bend.

130
00:07:16,360 --> 00:07:19,160
With the way the current runs, if we don't get to them quickly,

131
00:07:19,260 --> 00:07:20,560
they're going to be aground or they're

132
00:07:20,660 --> 00:07:23,400
going to be hitting one of the bridge abutments.

133
00:07:23,500 --> 00:07:25,400
Although patrol boats are able to do

134
00:07:25,401 --> 00:07:28,940
large commercial craft. Many private boats become

135
00:07:29,040 --> 00:07:32,200
distressed in the canal. The patrol boats handle

136
00:07:32,300 --> 00:07:36,440
about 200 and... Well, last year they handled 228 small boat

137
00:07:36,540 --> 00:07:41,780
assistance cases, and around 200 is the normal.

138
00:07:41,880 --> 00:07:44,820
Marine Operations also works closely with the Bay Colony

139
00:07:44,920 --> 00:07:47,760
Railroad operators on one of the more unique elements of the

140
00:07:47,860 --> 00:07:55,400
canal, the Vertical Lift Railroad Bridge.

141
00:07:55,401 --> 00:07:57,440
Most bridges of this type would normally

142
00:07:57,540 --> 00:07:59,940
be in a down position for railroad traffic.

143
00:08:00,040 --> 00:08:02,940
However, being the major waterway,

144
00:08:03,040 --> 00:08:06,940
the bridge is always left in the up position.

145
00:08:07,040 --> 00:08:09,840
Larry Janander has worked as one of the railroad bridge

146
00:08:09,940 --> 00:08:12,760
operators for 23 years.

147
00:08:12,860 --> 00:08:15,260
When the bridge needs to move, his is the voice

148
00:08:15,360 --> 00:08:17,560
that makes the first call.

149
00:08:17,660 --> 00:08:19,260
Traffic control.

150
00:08:19,360 --> 00:08:22,500
Luke, we're ready to make that move to check that G-brake

151
00:08:22,600 --> 00:08:24,200
problem with the PLC.

152
00:08:24,201 --> 00:08:25,400
OK, Larry.

153
00:08:25,500 --> 00:08:28,440
Whether it's a train or whether it's maintenance moves,

154
00:08:28,540 --> 00:08:30,500
we call the Army Corps of Engineers

155
00:08:30,600 --> 00:08:32,940
Marine Traffic Control Center.

156
00:08:33,040 --> 00:08:35,540
And the officer in charge, in turn,

157
00:08:35,640 --> 00:08:39,540
lets us know whether we can bring the bridge down or not.

158
00:08:39,640 --> 00:08:42,060
I have one westbound fishing vessel.

159
00:08:42,160 --> 00:08:43,060
Susie clears.

160
00:08:43,160 --> 00:08:43,960
I'll have the boat out.

161
00:08:44,060 --> 00:08:45,160
We'll drop it.

162
00:08:45,260 --> 00:08:47,960
When marine traffic control gives the OK,

163
00:08:48,060 --> 00:08:50,760
a patrol boat is sent out on the up current side of the bridge

164
00:08:50,860 --> 00:08:54,200
to stop any vessel traffic.

165
00:08:54,201 --> 00:08:58,863
The span is 544 feet. The span weighs 2,200 ton.

166
00:08:58,864 --> 00:09:03,720
We also have counterweights on the north and south

167
00:09:03,721 --> 00:09:07,607
sides that weigh 1,100 ton each, which gives you a balance of weight.

168
00:09:07,608 --> 00:09:09,240
Then with our total movement,

169
00:09:09,241 --> 00:09:14,920
we also have cables. There is 40 cables on the north side, 40 on the south side,

170
00:09:14,921 --> 00:09:19,800
and with those moving as well, we're moving actually around 4,800 ton.

171
00:09:24,200 --> 00:09:31,200
It takes approximately 2 1⁄2 minutes to lower the bridge

172
00:09:31,300 --> 00:09:35,440
from its home 135 feet above mean high tide.

173
00:09:38,620 --> 00:09:41,420
Vessels on the canal must stem the tide and wait for the patrol

174
00:09:41,520 --> 00:09:45,020
boat to give the okay for passage.

175
00:09:45,120 --> 00:09:49,400
There are two sets of 16-foot diameter shivs, four in each

176
00:09:49,500 --> 00:09:51,320
tower.

177
00:09:51,321 --> 00:09:53,560
Several small motors in each tower

178
00:09:53,660 --> 00:09:57,100
initiate the movement of the shivs, which in turn

179
00:09:57,200 --> 00:10:00,700
move the counterweights and the bridge fan.

180
00:10:04,700 --> 00:10:07,440
This is the machinery room.

181
00:10:07,540 --> 00:10:11,280
And what we have here that my hand is on is actually, as it

182
00:10:11,380 --> 00:10:15,880
says, a cooling fan for the 200 horsepower motor,

183
00:10:15,980 --> 00:10:18,440
which is motor A. And this motor

184
00:10:18,441 --> 00:10:22,440
And this motor is a 200-horsepower DC motor.

185
00:10:22,441 --> 00:10:27,120
And we have our B motor, which is also a 200-horsepower motor.

186
00:10:27,121 --> 00:10:31,680
The South Tower looks exactly like this, with two more motors.

187
00:10:31,681 --> 00:10:33,180
And there would be C and D motors.

188
00:10:36,660 --> 00:10:38,660
The Bay Colony Railroad train typically

189
00:10:38,661 --> 00:10:40,500
crosses the bridge four times a day.

190
00:10:45,100 --> 00:10:47,300
When the train has completed its crossing,

191
00:10:47,301 --> 00:10:50,300
minutes. The bridge operator raises the

192
00:10:50,301 --> 00:10:53,300
bridge and then lets marine traffic control know when the

193
00:10:53,301 --> 00:10:56,300
bridge span is locked in its up position.

194
00:10:59,300 --> 00:11:04,300
Vessel traffic can once again proceed under the bridge.

195
00:11:04,301 --> 00:11:07,300
But the workings of this canal and its bridges have not always

196
00:11:07,301 --> 00:11:11,300
been so finely tuned. The evolution of the canal was

197
00:11:11,301 --> 00:11:16,300
long, painful, and dangerous. The Sagamore and Bourne

198
00:11:16,301 --> 00:11:18,860
The Sagamore and Bourne bridges see a combined total of

199
00:11:18,861 --> 00:11:21,940
approximately 70,000 daily trips in winter.

200
00:11:21,941 --> 00:11:26,440
During the summer, the traffic nearly doubles with over 130,000

201
00:11:26,441 --> 00:11:32,740
daily trips. Cape Cod was formed thousands of

202
00:11:32,741 --> 00:11:38,360
years ago by Ice Age glaciers. It's home to fierce storms,

203
00:11:38,361 --> 00:11:41,600
deadly ice flows, and hidden shoals.

204
00:11:41,601 --> 00:11:46,540
The Great New England Hurricane of 1938 killed nearly 700.

205
00:11:46,640 --> 00:11:49,600
Top winds were clocked at 183 miles per hour,

206
00:11:49,700 --> 00:11:52,040
but it wasn't a unique event.

207
00:11:52,140 --> 00:11:55,740
Hurricanes have lashed this area every few years,

208
00:11:55,840 --> 00:12:00,060
but a possible safe haven for ships was always nearby.

209
00:12:00,160 --> 00:12:02,260
At the entrance to the Cape Cod Peninsula,

210
00:12:02,360 --> 00:12:06,100
a narrow isthmus separated two tidal rivers.

211
00:12:06,200 --> 00:12:08,000
On the northeast side of the isthmus,

212
00:12:08,100 --> 00:12:10,800
there was a vast salt marsh, where the Scusset River

213
00:12:10,801 --> 00:12:13,440
through. On the southwest side, the

214
00:12:13,441 --> 00:12:18,300
Monument River flowed inland nearly meeting the Scusset River.

215
00:12:18,301 --> 00:12:21,300
Between the two rivers was approximately a one-mile stretch

216
00:12:21,301 --> 00:12:24,940
of dry land. Captain Miles Standish of the

217
00:12:24,941 --> 00:12:28,000
Plymouth Colony first introduced the idea for a canal to cut

218
00:12:28,001 --> 00:12:33,100
across the Cape Cod Isthmus in 1623 to facilitate trade between

219
00:12:33,101 --> 00:12:37,700
the Native American Indians and the burgeoning colonies.

220
00:12:37,701 --> 00:12:40,360
The colonists' survival depended on their relationship with the

221
00:12:40,361 --> 00:12:44,600
Native American Indians. Standish knew the Indians walked

222
00:12:44,601 --> 00:12:47,600
their boats across the narrow isthmus.

223
00:12:47,601 --> 00:12:51,400
He wanted a water crossing. But with no way to implement such

224
00:12:51,401 --> 00:12:56,200
an undertaking, the idea of building a canal was abandoned

225
00:12:56,201 --> 00:13:01,720
until 1776, when George Washington envisioned a secure

226
00:13:01,721 --> 00:13:06,720
route for his navy battling the British.

227
00:13:06,721 --> 00:13:09,800
The British navy commanded the waters off the Cape, thus

228
00:13:09,801 --> 00:13:12,700
the small Continental Navy from passing.

229
00:13:12,701 --> 00:13:16,040
Washington's Navy moved goods and men by foot over the Isthmus

230
00:13:16,041 --> 00:13:20,040
to bypass the British Navy. But another century would pass

231
00:13:20,041 --> 00:13:24,440
before the canal Washington desired became a reality.

232
00:13:24,441 --> 00:13:27,960
As the country grew, shipping increased each year between the

233
00:13:27,961 --> 00:13:30,660
major ports of Boston and New York.

234
00:13:30,661 --> 00:13:34,500
We had all sorts of products in this country that were being

235
00:13:34,501 --> 00:13:38,500
shipped by a huge coastal industry that grew rapidly

236
00:13:38,501 --> 00:13:43,500
rapidly after the Civil War, and they would run across surging

237
00:13:43,501 --> 00:13:47,500
tides and ice flows that were very dangerous.

238
00:13:47,501 --> 00:13:50,500
Approximately 1,100 ships found it and were lost.

239
00:13:50,501 --> 00:13:54,500
Of this 1,100 ships, approximately 200 were complete

240
00:13:54,501 --> 00:13:58,500
losses, and there were about 150 lives lost.

241
00:13:58,501 --> 00:14:02,500
As the loss of life and cargo increased, so did attempts at

242
00:14:02,501 --> 00:14:06,500
building a canal in the Cape. With the success of the Erie

243
00:14:06,501 --> 00:14:10,500
nearly half a dozen companies tried but failed to conquer the

244
00:14:10,501 --> 00:14:15,500
small Cape Cod Isthmus. It wasn't until 1909 that

245
00:14:15,501 --> 00:14:18,500
technology and entrepreneurship in the form of August Belmont

246
00:14:18,501 --> 00:14:23,500
Jr. caught up with the notion of building a canal.

247
00:14:23,501 --> 00:14:26,500
August Belmont was born on Cape Cod and became famous for

248
00:14:26,501 --> 00:14:31,500
financing the first subway in New York City, completed in 1904.

249
00:14:31,501 --> 00:14:35,500
He was ready for his next project. He purchased a struggling

250
00:14:35,501 --> 00:14:39,800
a struggling company trying to build a canal on Cape Cod,

251
00:14:39,900 --> 00:14:43,140
the Boston, Cape Cod, and New York Canal Company.

252
00:14:43,240 --> 00:14:47,680
He always said he would complete the canal at any cost,

253
00:14:47,780 --> 00:14:50,020
and it was to protect Mariners.

254
00:14:50,120 --> 00:14:53,380
Interestingly enough, commerce was always the last thing

255
00:14:53,480 --> 00:14:54,620
he spoke about.

256
00:14:54,720 --> 00:14:57,260
It was always protection of the seamen,

257
00:14:57,360 --> 00:15:01,220
navigational rights for the military, and then commerce.

258
00:15:01,320 --> 00:15:04,200
Belmont immediately hired the renowned civil engineer

259
00:15:04,201 --> 00:15:08,200
William Barclay Parsons to be his chief engineer on the project.

260
00:15:09,200 --> 00:15:12,400
He had been one of those commissioned by Theodore Roosevelt

261
00:15:12,401 --> 00:15:17,120
originally to go down to survey the route of the Panama Canal.

262
00:15:18,260 --> 00:15:21,220
He had helped August Belmont build the 4th Avenue,

263
00:15:21,340 --> 00:15:22,700
the first subway in New York.

264
00:15:23,480 --> 00:15:26,800
And so he was a friend and also a professional.

265
00:15:26,801 --> 00:15:33,500
He was hired in 1905 to be in charge of the building of the Cape Cod Canal.

266
00:15:34,200 --> 00:15:39,500
Construction began in ceremonial fashion on June 22, 1909.

267
00:15:39,501 --> 00:15:44,580
Few believe this latest attempt at a canal would be successful.

268
00:15:44,581 --> 00:15:48,940
However, Belmont promised not to desert the task until the last

269
00:15:48,941 --> 00:15:54,860
shovelful has been dug. First, Parsons would try to

270
00:15:54,861 --> 00:15:58,260
construct a breakwater off the eastern end of what would become

271
00:15:58,261 --> 00:16:01,400
the canal. If he failed here, the canal would

272
00:16:01,401 --> 00:16:04,440
the canal would likely be doomed, as sand caught in the

273
00:16:04,540 --> 00:16:07,540
constantly moving currents would immediately fill any dredged

274
00:16:07,640 --> 00:16:11,140
channel. He used schooners to ship millions

275
00:16:11,240 --> 00:16:15,640
of tons of granite boulders from Maine to the breakwater site.

276
00:16:15,740 --> 00:16:19,060
He wouldn't stop until his breakwater was 40 feet deep,

277
00:16:19,160 --> 00:16:23,060
3,000 feet long, and rose 8 feet above high tide.

278
00:16:23,160 --> 00:16:27,200
It would take years. At the opposite end of the isthmus,

279
00:16:27,300 --> 00:16:31,400
in Buzzards Bay, dredges worked on the westerly approach.

280
00:16:31,401 --> 00:16:35,200
The recent Industrial Revolution had mechanized the ancient skill

281
00:16:35,201 --> 00:16:38,200
of dredging. The dredges at Parsons' disposal

282
00:16:38,201 --> 00:16:41,640
were much more powerful than any that had come before.

283
00:16:41,641 --> 00:16:44,640
The first dredge to arrive was the Kennedy.

284
00:16:44,641 --> 00:16:48,160
It arrived at the west end, and it was a ladder-type dredge.

285
00:16:48,161 --> 00:16:50,560
Now, a ladder-type dredge looks like a conveyor belt

286
00:16:50,561 --> 00:16:53,460
with little buckets on it. It goes in, and it digs away,

287
00:16:53,461 --> 00:16:56,700
digs away, and it expels the material to the rear onto a scow.

288
00:16:56,701 --> 00:16:59,700
The scow takes it away. Parsons' plan for dredging

289
00:16:59,701 --> 00:17:03,000
dredging would follow the paths of the rivers, simultaneously

290
00:17:03,100 --> 00:17:06,100
dredging from the east and west ends of the isthmus until the

291
00:17:06,200 --> 00:17:08,800
dredges presumed meeting in the middle.

292
00:17:08,900 --> 00:17:11,840
Although the plan was sound, Parsons was about to face an

293
00:17:11,940 --> 00:17:15,740
enormous problem, courtesy of the last ice age.

294
00:17:15,840 --> 00:17:19,560
From the beginning, Parsons knew that Cape Cod was a

295
00:17:19,660 --> 00:17:22,760
glacial moraine, a glacial dump, if you will.

296
00:17:22,860 --> 00:17:28,660
So he ran test borings, lots of them, and they went down 40, 50,

297
00:17:28,661 --> 00:17:32,360
down 40, 50 feet and never struck any boulders.

298
00:17:32,460 --> 00:17:36,600
But oh, did they find them when the dredges started digging.

299
00:17:36,700 --> 00:17:39,700
The only solution was to employ a new breed of worker

300
00:17:39,800 --> 00:17:45,240
to do a new kind of job, an extremely dangerous job.

301
00:17:45,340 --> 00:17:48,440
Dredging was halted so underwater divers could

302
00:17:48,540 --> 00:17:49,740
dynamite the obstructions.

303
00:17:53,080 --> 00:17:56,360
The 1820s had opened the seas to work and exploration

304
00:17:56,361 --> 00:18:00,360
with the development of diving pumps and underwater helmets.

305
00:18:00,460 --> 00:18:02,360
The equipment was heavy, and airflow

306
00:18:02,460 --> 00:18:04,760
had to be carefully monitored.

307
00:18:04,860 --> 00:18:07,100
Laboring for prolonged periods underwater

308
00:18:07,200 --> 00:18:10,800
was more perilous than any other work on the canal.

309
00:18:10,900 --> 00:18:14,320
Some of the rock at the west end exceeded 100 tons.

310
00:18:14,420 --> 00:18:16,240
The diving profession at that time

311
00:18:16,340 --> 00:18:19,880
wasn't what we would consider today to be the safest.

312
00:18:19,980 --> 00:18:21,960
The air pumps usually were run by hand.

313
00:18:22,060 --> 00:18:24,420
They were on leaky scouts.

314
00:18:24,421 --> 00:18:26,420
The divers were really brave,

315
00:18:27,060 --> 00:18:29,360
and they'd have to go down into very murky water,

316
00:18:29,900 --> 00:18:33,120
and they would blast anywhere from 7 1⁄2 pounds

317
00:18:33,121 --> 00:18:34,740
to 200 pounds of dynamite.

318
00:18:35,060 --> 00:18:36,140
It was very, very dangerous.

319
00:18:36,760 --> 00:18:38,660
Once they'd set the charge and have it ready,

320
00:18:38,820 --> 00:18:40,280
they'd have to get back up on the boat,

321
00:18:40,600 --> 00:18:42,600
back the boat off, blast.

322
00:18:43,240 --> 00:18:45,560
Once it's blasted, they'd have to go back down again

323
00:18:45,561 --> 00:18:48,180
to survey to see where they'd have to dig.

324
00:18:48,740 --> 00:18:51,620
And this became extremely time-consuming.

325
00:18:51,621 --> 00:18:54,860
Although dredging was severely delayed, work on the three

326
00:18:54,861 --> 00:18:57,860
bridges that Belmont was obligated to build as part of his

327
00:18:57,861 --> 00:19:01,860
canal charter progressed rapidly. There would be two highway

328
00:19:01,861 --> 00:19:05,860
bridges, the Bourne Bridge and Sagamore Bridge.

329
00:19:05,861 --> 00:19:08,860
They would combine space for a trolley line, and the dominant

330
00:19:08,861 --> 00:19:12,860
form of transportation at the time, horse and carriage.

331
00:19:12,861 --> 00:19:15,860
Both highway bridges would incorporate two 80-foot

332
00:19:15,861 --> 00:19:19,860
cantilever spans constructed of wood and steel.

333
00:19:19,861 --> 00:19:24,100
Each was completed in less than a year.

334
00:19:24,101 --> 00:19:28,100
Parsons decided on a different style for the third bridge, a

335
00:19:28,101 --> 00:19:32,800
railroad bridge. It would only have a single span.

336
00:19:32,801 --> 00:19:36,100
The railroad bridge had a single counterweight, which was on

337
00:19:36,101 --> 00:19:38,620
the north side. It was a bascule-type bridge,

338
00:19:38,621 --> 00:19:41,920
and so it would just open and close like that.

339
00:19:41,921 --> 00:19:45,920
By 1912, the bridges were finished, but the canal was

340
00:19:45,921 --> 00:19:48,560
not. Drastically behind schedule,

341
00:19:48,561 --> 00:19:52,560
Belmont and Parsons implemented a new plan to dig in the dry in

342
00:19:52,561 --> 00:19:57,560
the unexcavated center of the planned canal route.

343
00:19:57,561 --> 00:20:00,560
They moved in steam shovels, similar to those being used in

344
00:20:00,561 --> 00:20:04,560
the Panama Canal excavation, and set up a narrow gauge railroad

345
00:20:04,561 --> 00:20:09,560
to haul the debris away. Belmont's desire to get the job

346
00:20:09,561 --> 00:20:12,560
done at any cost resulted in his contracting for two

347
00:20:12,561 --> 00:20:16,560
specially built dipper dredges to be assembled on site at the

348
00:20:16,561 --> 00:20:20,800
on site at the west end and the east end of the canal.

349
00:20:20,900 --> 00:20:22,600
They were special because at the time,

350
00:20:22,700 --> 00:20:24,800
they were the biggest dredges in the country.

351
00:20:24,900 --> 00:20:27,600
These dredges were monsters.

352
00:20:27,700 --> 00:20:31,500
They had 13 steam engines in each one of them.

353
00:20:31,600 --> 00:20:34,520
They had a dipper dredge that was almost twice the size

354
00:20:34,620 --> 00:20:36,720
of any dipper dredge that was operating in the canal

355
00:20:36,820 --> 00:20:38,320
at that time.

356
00:20:38,420 --> 00:20:40,020
The two behemoths began digging

357
00:20:40,120 --> 00:20:44,060
toward each other in 1912.

358
00:20:44,061 --> 00:20:47,100
Accompanied by the trusty Kennedy ladder dredge and many

359
00:20:47,200 --> 00:20:51,400
smaller dipper dredges, Belmont was nearing his goal.

360
00:20:51,500 --> 00:20:55,640
There were approximately 26 dredges worked from the time of

361
00:20:55,740 --> 00:20:59,900
the first dredge that started to work around 1908 till the last

362
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:03,340
dredge pulled out of here in 1916.

363
00:21:03,440 --> 00:21:06,980
By the spring of 1914, only one sandy stretch of land

364
00:21:07,080 --> 00:21:11,120
separated the tidal rivers. Workers dug through the sand

365
00:21:11,121 --> 00:21:13,960
sand and constructed a wooden dike to keep the waters

366
00:21:13,961 --> 00:21:17,520
separated. In a ceremonial blending of the

367
00:21:17,521 --> 00:21:21,440
waters, Belmont and Parsons shook hands, lifted the dike's

368
00:21:21,441 --> 00:21:24,640
gate, and let the torrential waters flood the canal area.

369
00:21:25,360 --> 00:21:27,980
Dredges would soon finish the job of widening and deepening

370
00:21:27,981 --> 00:21:32,840
the final stretch of land, and on July 29, 1914, the Cape Cod

371
00:21:32,841 --> 00:21:34,840
Canal opened with great fanfare.

372
00:21:34,841 --> 00:21:40,840
20 million cubic yards of material had been removed, and

373
00:21:40,841 --> 00:21:46,360
Cape Cod had become an island. The canal had cost Belmont

374
00:21:46,361 --> 00:21:50,280
$16 million. His plan was to charge tolls for

375
00:21:50,281 --> 00:21:53,960
all vessels using the canal. With schooner barges carrying

376
00:21:53,961 --> 00:21:57,400
coal and large passenger steamships coming in droves, he

377
00:21:57,401 --> 00:22:00,600
was certain his canal would be a financial success.

378
00:22:00,601 --> 00:22:04,840
However, it soon became clear that the canal was too small.

379
00:22:04,841 --> 00:22:07,740
Although Parsons had conducted extensive studies,

380
00:22:07,840 --> 00:22:12,080
the canal depth of 25 feet and width of 140 feet

381
00:22:12,180 --> 00:22:13,580
proved to be inadequate.

382
00:22:13,680 --> 00:22:16,960
There wasn't enough room in the canal for vessels to maneuver.

383
00:22:17,060 --> 00:22:20,160
Furthermore, the narrow width actually increased the velocity

384
00:22:20,260 --> 00:22:22,660
of the current.

385
00:22:22,760 --> 00:22:25,500
One of the problems was the cross rips

386
00:22:25,600 --> 00:22:27,260
and when the tide changed.

387
00:22:27,360 --> 00:22:32,540
If a pilot who was captaining the ship through the canal

388
00:22:32,541 --> 00:22:36,740
wasn't on his game, he would find that he'd be into the shore

389
00:22:36,840 --> 00:22:38,280
very quickly.

390
00:22:38,380 --> 00:22:40,140
It was difficult to navigate.

391
00:22:40,240 --> 00:22:42,180
The approach channel through Buzzards Bay

392
00:22:42,280 --> 00:22:44,980
took a series of sharp turns, and in combination

393
00:22:45,080 --> 00:22:48,780
with the strong currents, made many mariners weary.

394
00:22:48,880 --> 00:22:51,300
The shipwrecks started to accumulate.

395
00:22:51,400 --> 00:22:53,800
Vessels couldn't navigate the strong current

396
00:22:53,900 --> 00:22:55,600
and would crash into one of the bridges

397
00:22:55,700 --> 00:22:58,900
or run aground on the riprap, erosion protection rock

398
00:22:59,000 --> 00:23:02,540
that lines the shores.

399
00:23:02,541 --> 00:23:06,080
Each time there was a wreck, the canal had to close to traffic,

400
00:23:06,081 --> 00:23:08,700
which meant a loss of revenue and additional trepidation

401
00:23:08,701 --> 00:23:12,040
in ship captains. Ships were soon taking chances

402
00:23:12,041 --> 00:23:15,780
with the old sea route around the Cape instead of paying the toll

403
00:23:15,781 --> 00:23:18,780
and risking the unpredictable canal.

404
00:23:18,781 --> 00:23:22,300
Belmont's Grand Canal, one which no one else had been able to

405
00:23:22,301 --> 00:23:25,100
build, was a financial failure.

406
00:23:25,101 --> 00:23:29,900
Could Belmont find a way to get off his sinking ship?

407
00:23:29,901 --> 00:23:33,660
visionary August Belmont Jr. built Belmont Park Racetrack,

408
00:23:33,661 --> 00:23:37,100
home to the Belmont Stakes, and helped build two America's Cup

409
00:23:37,101 --> 00:23:42,140
winning yachts. Today's Cape Cod Canal is far

410
00:23:42,141 --> 00:23:47,660
different than the one August Belmont built a century ago.

411
00:23:47,661 --> 00:23:53,660
His canal was plagued by racing tidal surges and shipwrecks.

412
00:23:53,661 --> 00:23:57,260
Although he periodically dredged the canal, it was fundamentally

413
00:23:57,261 --> 00:24:01,060
flawed. Up until his death in 1924,

414
00:24:01,061 --> 00:24:04,360
Belmont tried to rid himself of the canal, which had proven to

415
00:24:04,361 --> 00:24:07,400
be his money pit. Belmont's canal was an

416
00:24:07,401 --> 00:24:10,600
economic failure. The amount of traffic they saw

417
00:24:10,601 --> 00:24:15,720
in the canal was much less than what investors had anticipated.

418
00:24:15,721 --> 00:24:18,620
There was only one party interested in purchasing

419
00:24:18,621 --> 00:24:22,620
the canal. In March of 1928, the canal was

420
00:24:22,621 --> 00:24:27,260
finally sold to the federal government for $11.4 million.

421
00:24:27,261 --> 00:24:33,500
$4.6 million less than what it cost Belmont to build.

422
00:24:33,501 --> 00:24:36,940
Weeks later, Congress handed the Cape Cod Canal over to the U.S.

423
00:24:36,941 --> 00:24:41,100
Army Corps of Engineers to operate and improve.

424
00:24:41,101 --> 00:24:44,380
Passage would be free to the maritime public.

425
00:24:44,381 --> 00:24:46,520
A lot of people wonder why the Corps of Engineers is here

426
00:24:46,521 --> 00:24:47,720
at the canal.

427
00:24:47,721 --> 00:24:51,120
The Corps of Engineers nationwide has a civil works function

428
00:24:51,121 --> 00:24:54,920
where we perform work as authorized by Congress.

429
00:24:54,921 --> 00:24:58,520
And that's how the Corps got here to the canal.

430
00:24:58,521 --> 00:25:02,420
Congress made the decision to purchase the then private canal

431
00:25:02,421 --> 00:25:05,780
and turn it into a viable waterway.

432
00:25:05,781 --> 00:25:09,020
The Corps worked with shipping companies, other maritime users

433
00:25:09,021 --> 00:25:11,260
of the canal, and local businesses

434
00:25:11,261 --> 00:25:15,080
to discern the major problems that plagued the passage.

435
00:25:15,081 --> 00:25:18,080
They then went forward with a whole series of surveys,

436
00:25:18,081 --> 00:25:23,220
what they could do to improve the condition of the canal,

437
00:25:23,221 --> 00:25:27,060
what they could do to improve the navigability of the canal,

438
00:25:27,061 --> 00:25:31,220
the safety requirements, and what did they want the canal

439
00:25:31,221 --> 00:25:33,860
to ultimately be.

440
00:25:33,861 --> 00:25:37,720
All the problems the canal had came down to one cause.

441
00:25:37,721 --> 00:25:41,040
It was just too small.

442
00:25:41,041 --> 00:25:43,480
As the Army Corps of Engineers was drawing up plans

443
00:25:43,481 --> 00:25:46,820
for this major waterway project, FDR's New Deal

444
00:25:46,821 --> 00:25:49,020
gave it the push that it needed.

445
00:25:49,021 --> 00:25:52,160
In December 1933, the federal government

446
00:25:52,161 --> 00:25:57,160
nearly $6 million to widen the canal and build three bigger and

447
00:25:57,161 --> 00:26:02,160
better bridges. Belmont's bridges would simply

448
00:26:02,161 --> 00:26:06,160
be obstacles in a wider canal. The government decided we

449
00:26:06,161 --> 00:26:11,160
will make a WPA project, a federal work project, out of

450
00:26:11,161 --> 00:26:13,160
this. And they said we will make this

451
00:26:13,161 --> 00:26:17,160
into a modern canal. It meant that 400 local men,

452
00:26:17,161 --> 00:26:20,160
heads of households, they had to be married men with families,

453
00:26:20,161 --> 00:26:22,560
were hired by the WPA.

454
00:26:22,561 --> 00:26:25,400
And it made it so that if you wanted a job,

455
00:26:25,401 --> 00:26:26,700
you could get a job.

456
00:26:26,701 --> 00:26:30,300
The pay wasn't great, but at least you were working.

457
00:26:30,301 --> 00:26:33,500
And it added to the economy of the Commonwealth.

458
00:26:33,501 --> 00:26:35,700
The first order of business was to begin work

459
00:26:35,701 --> 00:26:37,420
on the bridges.

460
00:26:37,421 --> 00:26:40,120
The old bridges were still open and still operating

461
00:26:40,121 --> 00:26:42,320
for the railroad and the two highway bridges

462
00:26:42,321 --> 00:26:45,520
until the new bridges were completed.

463
00:26:45,521 --> 00:26:47,960
While the old bridges were drawbridge style,

464
00:26:47,961 --> 00:26:50,800
the two new highway bridges were both planned to be fixed

465
00:26:50,900 --> 00:26:52,300
bridges.

466
00:26:52,400 --> 00:26:54,400
They would be designed to provide enough clearance

467
00:26:54,500 --> 00:26:58,300
for the tallest of ocean-going vessels.

468
00:26:58,400 --> 00:27:03,300
Simultaneous construction began on all three bridges in 1933.

469
00:27:03,400 --> 00:27:06,320
With a dedicated workforce, the Sagamore and Bourne Bridge

470
00:27:06,420 --> 00:27:08,920
construction moved swiftly.

471
00:27:09,020 --> 00:27:11,220
The differences between the two highway bridges

472
00:27:11,320 --> 00:27:14,060
are just the length of the approach spans.

473
00:27:14,160 --> 00:27:16,520
Most people think of them as being identical,

474
00:27:16,521 --> 00:27:20,260
But the Bourne Bridge is longer than the Sagamore Bridge.

475
00:27:20,360 --> 00:27:23,860
The Bourne Bridge is 2,384 feet long,

476
00:27:23,960 --> 00:27:29,160
and the Sagamore is 1,408 feet long.

477
00:27:29,260 --> 00:27:32,260
The highway bridges are elevated in design.

478
00:27:32,360 --> 00:27:36,580
They allow for a clearance of 135 feet from the bottom of the

479
00:27:36,680 --> 00:27:39,980
bridge to the top of the water at mean high tide.

480
00:27:40,080 --> 00:27:43,080
The engineers made the new bridges four lanes wide

481
00:27:43,081 --> 00:27:47,320
instead of two, and 40 feet from curb to curb, more than 10

482
00:27:47,420 --> 00:27:49,020
feet wider than Belmont's bridges.

483
00:27:51,720 --> 00:27:54,820
Workers completed the Bourne and Sagamore bridges just two years

484
00:27:54,920 --> 00:27:56,320
after the start of construction.

485
00:27:59,840 --> 00:28:02,340
But one bridge remained to be built.

486
00:28:02,440 --> 00:28:05,440
The most difficult was to build the railway bridge.

487
00:28:05,540 --> 00:28:08,740
They had to go in and engineer the whole project so that

488
00:28:08,840 --> 00:28:12,340
everything went together like a fine-geared meshed machine.

489
00:28:12,341 --> 00:28:15,580
The original Bascule Railroad Bridge was too small

490
00:28:15,680 --> 00:28:17,580
to span the new canal.

491
00:28:17,680 --> 00:28:20,280
It was also built to remain in the lowered position

492
00:28:20,380 --> 00:28:22,680
until a boat needed to pass.

493
00:28:22,780 --> 00:28:25,460
This would impede the goal of the new canal, which

494
00:28:25,560 --> 00:28:28,320
was to always have an open waterway.

495
00:28:28,420 --> 00:28:30,920
So the Army Corps plans called for the construction

496
00:28:31,020 --> 00:28:33,600
of a vertical lift railroad bridge,

497
00:28:33,700 --> 00:28:36,740
meaning the entire center span could be raised and lowered

498
00:28:36,840 --> 00:28:37,840
as needed.

499
00:28:37,841 --> 00:28:40,340
needed.

500
00:28:40,440 --> 00:28:42,320
This ambitious proposition gave rise

501
00:28:42,420 --> 00:28:45,080
to the longest vertical lift railroad bridge in the world

502
00:28:45,180 --> 00:28:47,620
at the time.

503
00:28:47,720 --> 00:28:53,020
The movable bridge span would cover a distance of 544 feet.

504
00:28:53,120 --> 00:28:56,220
Such a large movable span meant that they could link the two

505
00:28:56,320 --> 00:29:01,000
sides of the proposed 500-foot wide canal.

506
00:29:01,100 --> 00:29:03,260
The way the railroad bridge was constructed,

507
00:29:03,360 --> 00:29:06,240
as with all the bridges along the canal,

508
00:29:06,241 --> 00:29:15,933
There were two teams, one team on the mainland side and one team on the Cape side, and they would build simultaneously and then meet in the middle, which is an amazing

509
00:29:15,934 --> 00:29:16,920
engineering feat.

510
00:29:17,420 --> 00:29:24,360
This method of construction allowed the canal to stay open for vessel traffic and required most of the work to be done on dry land.

511
00:29:25,500 --> 00:29:28,140
Foundation work began with the building of cofferdams.

512
00:29:28,580 --> 00:29:31,840
This would allow a dry work site for construction of the bridge piers.

513
00:29:32,940 --> 00:29:36,020
They had to go down 62 feet below sea level.

514
00:29:36,240 --> 00:29:40,080
But they ran into the exact same problems that August Belmont

515
00:29:40,180 --> 00:29:44,180
and Parsons had run into, boulders.

516
00:29:44,280 --> 00:29:46,580
Engineers suggested an ingenious solution

517
00:29:46,680 --> 00:29:49,480
to deal with boulders.

518
00:29:49,580 --> 00:29:55,900
Revised designs called for 324 oak piles, 38 to 40 feet long,

519
00:29:56,000 --> 00:29:59,700
and capable of bearing 30 tons each.

520
00:29:59,800 --> 00:30:01,700
These piles were driven into the ground,

521
00:30:01,800 --> 00:30:06,240
surrounded by the cofferdams, until they hit boulders.

522
00:30:06,241 --> 00:30:11,340
an average distance of 45 feet below mean sea level.

523
00:30:11,440 --> 00:30:14,040
The cofferdams were then filled with concrete,

524
00:30:14,140 --> 00:30:16,680
and the channel piers were subsequently built on top

525
00:30:16,780 --> 00:30:19,580
of these sturdy pier basins.

526
00:30:19,680 --> 00:30:24,700
This was a monumental task, and it was beautifully done.

527
00:30:24,800 --> 00:30:27,300
They had to go down to this great depth with the piers

528
00:30:27,400 --> 00:30:30,140
because of the weight, not only of the railway tracks,

529
00:30:30,240 --> 00:30:33,240
but of the superstructure that was there.

530
00:30:33,340 --> 00:30:36,240
This substructure was completed on the Buzzards Bay Railway

531
00:30:36,241 --> 00:30:41,040
bridge in January 1935. That same year in March, work

532
00:30:41,041 --> 00:30:46,080
began on the superstructure, the steelwork of the bridge.

533
00:30:46,081 --> 00:30:51,260
Workers used over 4,000 tons of steel and approximately 110,000

534
00:30:51,261 --> 00:30:54,260
steel rivets in constructing the bridge.

535
00:30:54,261 --> 00:30:57,860
Now the core had a problem. It needed to close the canal

536
00:30:57,861 --> 00:31:00,400
during construction of the bridge span, but wanted to

537
00:31:00,401 --> 00:31:03,260
minimize the time of the closure.

538
00:31:03,261 --> 00:31:08,260
Though not successful, the canal was still a benefit to mariners.

539
00:31:08,360 --> 00:31:11,500
The Army Corps would build the bridge span in sections, then

540
00:31:11,600 --> 00:31:17,000
rapidly assemble them. Engineers worked with 18 panels,

541
00:31:17,100 --> 00:31:21,340
divided into three sections. At roadway level, workers

542
00:31:21,440 --> 00:31:24,740
assembled one section of six panels from the north tower,

543
00:31:24,840 --> 00:31:28,220
then another section of six panels from the south.

544
00:31:28,320 --> 00:31:31,620
The third section, or midsection, linked the other two

545
00:31:31,621 --> 00:31:36,220
and completed the bridge. This process took 90 workers

546
00:31:36,221 --> 00:31:40,460
only five days to complete. With their fingers crossed,

547
00:31:40,461 --> 00:31:43,860
engineers successfully raised the center span of the world's

548
00:31:43,861 --> 00:31:48,980
widest vertical lift bridge on September 16, 1935.

549
00:31:48,981 --> 00:31:52,380
Unlike most railroad bridges, the span here at the canal

550
00:31:52,381 --> 00:31:56,780
is kept in the raised position. Again, that's because navigation

551
00:31:56,781 --> 00:32:01,620
has the right of way. With a crew of five workers,

552
00:32:01,621 --> 00:32:04,920
the four motors triggered the movement of the shivs, activating

553
00:32:04,921 --> 00:32:07,920
the movement of the two counterweights, and the span was

554
00:32:07,921 --> 00:32:10,860
successfully lowered for the first train crossing in December

555
00:32:10,861 --> 00:32:15,360
1935. Now began the task of widening

556
00:32:15,361 --> 00:32:18,780
the canal. It was up to the engineers to

557
00:32:18,781 --> 00:32:22,180
harness the forces of the tides and create a safe waterway for

558
00:32:22,181 --> 00:32:25,580
all types of vessels, but could they truly improve upon

559
00:32:25,581 --> 00:32:30,580
Belmont's and Parsons' efforts? Some locals wanted the canal to

560
00:32:30,581 --> 00:32:32,980
to be filled in when the Army Corps of Engineers

561
00:32:33,080 --> 00:32:37,080
took it over in 1928. The Big Ditch, as some called it,

562
00:32:37,180 --> 00:32:39,380
actually split the town of Bourne in half.

563
00:32:43,820 --> 00:32:46,620
The uncompromising Atlantic around Cape Cod

564
00:32:46,720 --> 00:32:48,340
has garnered the respect of mariners

565
00:32:48,440 --> 00:32:52,140
since the first vessel sailed her waters.

566
00:32:52,240 --> 00:32:55,780
Both the Gulf Stream and the Labrador Current run nearby

567
00:32:55,880 --> 00:32:58,340
and affect ocean waters that swirl around the Cape.

568
00:33:00,580 --> 00:33:03,580
These currents, combined with the contrasting tide levels

569
00:33:03,680 --> 00:33:07,080
on opposite ends of the canal, create the rapid flow of water

570
00:33:07,180 --> 00:33:09,180
that alternates direction with each new tide.

571
00:33:12,260 --> 00:33:14,800
The current will run to the west for six hours,

572
00:33:14,900 --> 00:33:16,460
and then it will go into slack water.

573
00:33:16,560 --> 00:33:18,540
And we have like 15, 20 minutes of slack water

574
00:33:18,640 --> 00:33:20,560
where the current isn't really moving at all.

575
00:33:20,660 --> 00:33:22,040
And then the current changes direction,

576
00:33:22,140 --> 00:33:24,240
and it will run the other direction for six hours.

577
00:33:24,340 --> 00:33:27,480
So every day there's two east currents, two west currents.

578
00:33:30,580 --> 00:33:34,180
In the 1930s, engineers faced the daunting challenge

579
00:33:34,280 --> 00:33:37,220
of transforming this unusual and potentially hazardous

580
00:33:37,320 --> 00:33:41,320
waterway into a safe and navigable canal.

581
00:33:41,420 --> 00:33:43,920
One possibility was to build locks,

582
00:33:44,020 --> 00:33:46,520
which had proven successful in the massive Panama Canal

583
00:33:46,620 --> 00:33:48,240
project.

584
00:33:48,340 --> 00:33:50,340
Navigation locks are gated enclosures

585
00:33:50,440 --> 00:33:53,300
that vessels enter. They then fill with water

586
00:33:53,400 --> 00:33:56,180
so that the vessel may pass from level to level.

587
00:33:56,280 --> 00:33:59,740
Thus, engineers can still raging currents.

588
00:33:59,741 --> 00:34:03,580
Parsons said that it would never need locks.

589
00:34:03,680 --> 00:34:06,040
Now, you have to remember that Parsons also had said

590
00:34:06,140 --> 00:34:08,380
at Panama you don't need locks.

591
00:34:08,480 --> 00:34:12,080
One of the primary reasons was it would slow transportation

592
00:34:12,180 --> 00:34:13,980
through the canal.

593
00:34:14,080 --> 00:34:16,160
The very simple reason for not building locks

594
00:34:16,260 --> 00:34:19,500
was that they wanted to keep this as an open seaway.

595
00:34:19,600 --> 00:34:21,960
The experience in the north with canals,

596
00:34:22,060 --> 00:34:24,400
all sorts of canals in Pennsylvania, Virginia,

597
00:34:24,500 --> 00:34:27,400
New York State, like the Erie Canal and so forth,

598
00:34:27,401 --> 00:34:31,400
that we had, and many here in Massachusetts too, would freeze

599
00:34:31,500 --> 00:34:35,400
in the wintertime. The U.S. Army Corps of Engineers

600
00:34:35,500 --> 00:34:39,140
hired the Massachusetts Institute of Technology to build a model

601
00:34:39,240 --> 00:34:41,740
of the canal with modifiable dimensions.

602
00:34:41,840 --> 00:34:45,760
Scientists and engineers studied tides and water levels created

603
00:34:45,860 --> 00:34:48,760
by an electrical water control system.

604
00:34:48,860 --> 00:34:52,760
The hydraulic data provided the Army Corps with solid facts on

605
00:34:52,860 --> 00:34:55,400
which to determine the dimensions of the canal.

606
00:34:55,401 --> 00:34:59,400
on which to determine the dimensions of the canal.

607
00:34:59,500 --> 00:35:03,000
The MIT data also illuminated serious cross-current and eddy

608
00:35:03,100 --> 00:35:06,540
conditions in the approach channel within Buzzards Bay

609
00:35:06,640 --> 00:35:10,840
that could create desperate conditions for ocean vessels.

610
00:35:10,940 --> 00:35:14,360
Engineers decided to build dikes composed of dredged material

611
00:35:14,460 --> 00:35:16,760
along the approach to the Buzzards Bay entrance of the

612
00:35:16,860 --> 00:35:19,560
canal.

613
00:35:19,660 --> 00:35:22,760
Because dikes are barriers built to redirect water,

614
00:35:22,761 --> 00:35:25,760
They would help reduce the swift current and lessen sand

615
00:35:25,761 --> 00:35:29,360
buildup. The new dikes would also

616
00:35:29,361 --> 00:35:33,200
eliminate a few very sharp turns in Buzzards Bay that had proven

617
00:35:33,201 --> 00:35:36,400
disastrous in Belmont's canal. With modern dredging

618
00:35:36,401 --> 00:35:40,920
equipment and modern dynamiting equipment, they made a straight

619
00:35:40,921 --> 00:35:44,720
channel into the west end of the canal.

620
00:35:44,721 --> 00:35:48,320
They chopped Hog Island, which was right in the middle of the

621
00:35:48,321 --> 00:35:52,560
the entrance in Buzz's Bay, right in half.

622
00:35:52,660 --> 00:35:55,300
The Army Corps began a massive dredging operation

623
00:35:55,400 --> 00:35:58,100
to widen, deepen, and adjust the entire canal

624
00:35:58,200 --> 00:36:01,200
and its approaches.

625
00:36:01,300 --> 00:36:04,100
Engineers also needed to prevent the sandy banks of the canal

626
00:36:04,200 --> 00:36:06,400
from eroding.

627
00:36:06,500 --> 00:36:10,740
Riprap was placed on both sides of the seven-mile land cut.

628
00:36:10,840 --> 00:36:12,840
Riprap lines the sides of the canal,

629
00:36:12,940 --> 00:36:16,820
and it's granite rock that's placed along the banks.

630
00:36:16,821 --> 00:36:19,860
And it's placed there to keep wakes of vessels

631
00:36:19,960 --> 00:36:23,960
from washing the banks out and also the strong current

632
00:36:24,060 --> 00:36:26,700
from making the banks erode.

633
00:36:26,800 --> 00:36:29,800
About 54 million cubic yards of material

634
00:36:29,900 --> 00:36:32,340
were removed for the Cape Cod Canal.

635
00:36:32,440 --> 00:36:34,580
About 15 million cubic yards of that

636
00:36:34,680 --> 00:36:36,900
was during Belmont's Canal, and the rest

637
00:36:37,000 --> 00:36:38,740
during the reconstruction.

638
00:36:38,840 --> 00:36:40,740
The entire restoration of the canal,

639
00:36:40,840 --> 00:36:43,840
including the construction of the three new bridges,

640
00:36:43,940 --> 00:36:46,820
took only seven years.

641
00:36:46,821 --> 00:36:50,820
Completed in 1940, the new and improved canal was to become a

642
00:36:50,920 --> 00:36:53,860
saving grace to the maritime community, especially the

643
00:36:53,960 --> 00:36:57,300
military, preparing for an impending war.

644
00:36:57,400 --> 00:37:01,060
During World War II, the US Navy found the Cape Cod Canal

645
00:37:01,160 --> 00:37:04,440
an essential passage in which to skirt the lingering enemy

646
00:37:04,540 --> 00:37:08,240
submarines positioned off the eastern seaboard.

647
00:37:08,340 --> 00:37:11,920
Convoys of military vessels from New York carrying troops and

648
00:37:12,020 --> 00:37:14,220
supplies moved through the canal.

649
00:37:14,221 --> 00:37:16,220
Messels would anchor in Buzzards Bay,

650
00:37:16,320 --> 00:37:18,720
awaiting the okay to pass through.

651
00:37:18,820 --> 00:37:23,560
The Cape Cod Canal made a major reputation for itself

652
00:37:23,660 --> 00:37:26,260
with the government forces fighting the war,

653
00:37:26,360 --> 00:37:30,660
because what we had at Buzzards Bay was a staging area.

654
00:37:30,760 --> 00:37:34,280
And the staging area was protected by minefields

655
00:37:34,380 --> 00:37:37,280
to make sure the German submarines couldn't get through,

656
00:37:37,380 --> 00:37:39,680
because the great threat in the oceans, of course,

657
00:37:39,780 --> 00:37:42,780
was the German undersea boats.

658
00:37:42,781 --> 00:37:47,020
Over the next seven decades, the canal would endure wars,

659
00:37:47,120 --> 00:37:49,880
weather, and a growing population.

660
00:37:49,980 --> 00:37:52,660
It would also incorporate burgeoning technology

661
00:37:52,760 --> 00:37:57,200
to remain a vital component of maritime transport.

662
00:37:57,300 --> 00:38:00,360
The canal has successfully stood the test of time,

663
00:38:00,460 --> 00:38:03,260
but will the mass of humanity that travels over the two

664
00:38:03,360 --> 00:38:07,480
highway bridges each year and invades the shore every season

665
00:38:07,580 --> 00:38:12,780
become too great a burden for an aging infrastructure?

666
00:38:12,781 --> 00:38:16,480
In the first seven months after the US entered World War II,

667
00:38:16,481 --> 00:38:20,780
five Allied ships were sunk by German U-boats within 100 miles

668
00:38:20,781 --> 00:38:27,320
of the Cape Cod Canal. The new millennium has introduced

669
00:38:27,321 --> 00:38:30,940
new technology and new fears to almost every facet of the

670
00:38:30,941 --> 00:38:36,240
country's infrastructure. Cape Cod Canal is no exception.

671
00:38:36,241 --> 00:38:39,740
Today, all three Cape Cod Canal bridges are monitored with

672
00:38:39,741 --> 00:38:44,340
cameras on a 24-7 basis from the same control center that

673
00:38:44,341 --> 00:38:47,040
monitors the canal itself.

674
00:38:47,041 --> 00:38:51,280
Since 9-11, Homeland Security has affected any project that

675
00:38:51,281 --> 00:38:53,480
the Corps has with a lot of infrastructure, and of course,

676
00:38:53,481 --> 00:38:54,780
it's affected us.

677
00:38:54,781 --> 00:38:59,000
Now we have electronic equipment that can see places around the

678
00:38:59,001 --> 00:39:02,600
bridges that we never monitored remotely before.

679
00:39:02,601 --> 00:39:04,900
We're keeping an eye on things a lot closer than we ever did

680
00:39:04,901 --> 00:39:07,940
before, just like everybody else is.

681
00:39:07,941 --> 00:39:11,240
Along with security monitoring, all three bridges are routinely

682
00:39:11,340 --> 00:39:13,540
inspected and maintained.

683
00:39:13,640 --> 00:39:16,440
Maintenance of the bridge is continuous.

684
00:39:16,540 --> 00:39:18,680
There's always something in it that needs doing.

685
00:39:18,780 --> 00:39:22,380
The Boren Bridge is right now in the process of being painted.

686
00:39:22,480 --> 00:39:26,900
The process of removing the paint is quite complicated.

687
00:39:27,000 --> 00:39:28,900
Everything has to be contained.

688
00:39:29,000 --> 00:39:32,540
We wind up setting up a whole system of containment

689
00:39:32,640 --> 00:39:36,540
to stop any of the painting debris from getting to the canal

690
00:39:36,541 --> 00:39:39,100
to the canal or getting to the local grounds.

691
00:39:39,101 --> 00:39:42,040
Suspended from the structure cells,

692
00:39:42,041 --> 00:39:43,140
we have the steel decking.

693
00:39:43,141 --> 00:39:46,980
That's the bottom portion of our containment.

694
00:39:46,981 --> 00:39:50,380
The tarps that we stretch along the sides of the bridge

695
00:39:50,381 --> 00:39:54,000
from the curb line all the way down to the steel decking,

696
00:39:54,001 --> 00:39:59,000
that will fully enclose the area that we will be sandblasting.

697
00:39:59,001 --> 00:40:01,500
Total containment of the area being sandblasted

698
00:40:01,501 --> 00:40:06,000
is a necessity because the old lead paint is toxic.

699
00:40:06,001 --> 00:40:07,700
This is an area of the bridge that we haven't

700
00:40:07,701 --> 00:40:10,880
did any paint work on yet this year.

701
00:40:10,881 --> 00:40:12,440
And if you look over here, you can see

702
00:40:12,441 --> 00:40:15,380
the typical rust areas.

703
00:40:15,381 --> 00:40:19,480
And as I said before, this is a lead removal project.

704
00:40:19,481 --> 00:40:23,420
And any time you see the orange here in that paint,

705
00:40:23,421 --> 00:40:26,380
you know that that's the red lead climber.

706
00:40:26,381 --> 00:40:30,100
And that's what we have to take all that off.

707
00:40:30,101 --> 00:40:34,960
And the paint there is, oh, it's pretty thick.

708
00:40:34,961 --> 00:40:37,260
And we've got 26 mils there of paint,

709
00:40:37,261 --> 00:40:38,460
so we've got a lot of work to do.

710
00:40:41,300 --> 00:40:43,460
All right, now we're on the other side of the containment

711
00:40:43,461 --> 00:40:46,640
in the area of the bridge that was painted about four or five

712
00:40:46,641 --> 00:40:49,280
months ago before the winter set in.

713
00:40:49,281 --> 00:40:51,340
And you can see there's quite a difference

714
00:40:51,341 --> 00:40:52,640
in the quality of the paint.

715
00:40:57,540 --> 00:40:59,760
Painting and maintenance on the two fixed bridges

716
00:40:59,761 --> 00:41:01,220
are one thing.

717
00:41:01,221 --> 00:41:03,780
But on the movable span of the railroad bridge,

718
00:41:03,781 --> 00:41:05,820
it's far more difficult.

719
00:41:05,920 --> 00:41:08,460
Within the last few years, we've renovated this bridge.

720
00:41:08,560 --> 00:41:13,260
It's taken about three years and $25, $26 million

721
00:41:13,360 --> 00:41:14,160
to renovate it.

722
00:41:14,260 --> 00:41:15,520
The first portion of the renovation

723
00:41:15,620 --> 00:41:19,060
was a complete lead paint removal project.

724
00:41:19,160 --> 00:41:21,300
That lasted for approximately two years.

725
00:41:21,400 --> 00:41:23,760
And then we had a mechanical rehab that lasted for,

726
00:41:23,860 --> 00:41:25,400
well, close to a year.

727
00:41:25,500 --> 00:41:28,280
During the mechanical rehab, we had the bridge out of service

728
00:41:28,380 --> 00:41:29,480
for only three months.

729
00:41:29,481 --> 00:41:37,620
And the reason that was difficult is that we're an active navigation project, and we

730
00:41:37,621 --> 00:41:42,540
had to keep the canal open to navigation, so the span had to be in the up position.

731
00:41:42,541 --> 00:41:45,720
Now that's just the opposite of how the bridge was built.

732
00:41:45,721 --> 00:41:49,240
The bridge was built with the span and the down position.

733
00:41:49,241 --> 00:41:54,360
So this time, when we did the rehab, we had to lock the span in the up position.

734
00:41:54,361 --> 00:41:59,020
Supports had to be installed from the water piers up to the underside of the span to hold

735
00:41:59,021 --> 00:42:01,260
it in place.

736
00:42:01,261 --> 00:42:03,220
We had to jack up the counterweights

737
00:42:03,221 --> 00:42:05,560
to release the tension on the cables

738
00:42:05,561 --> 00:42:07,300
and then change out the cables.

739
00:42:07,301 --> 00:42:11,300
While we were doing that, we removed the shivs up

740
00:42:11,301 --> 00:42:14,260
in the wheelhouse.

741
00:42:14,261 --> 00:42:16,400
We stripped the electrical and mechanical guts out

742
00:42:16,401 --> 00:42:18,940
of the bridge for the first time in 70 years

743
00:42:18,941 --> 00:42:25,240
and replaced it all so that we could minimize the impact

744
00:42:25,241 --> 00:42:27,980
of having the bridge out of service.

745
00:42:27,981 --> 00:42:30,640
Right now, these bridges are over 70 years old.

746
00:42:31,560 --> 00:42:34,820
We expect that they'll last at least another 50,

747
00:42:34,920 --> 00:42:36,480
maybe even another 70 years old.

748
00:42:36,980 --> 00:42:40,220
With proper maintenance, we expect these bridges

749
00:42:40,221 --> 00:42:43,020
will easily stand up for the test of time.

750
00:42:46,300 --> 00:42:48,880
In addition to overseeing bridge maintenance

751
00:42:48,881 --> 00:42:51,700
and marine traffic, the Army Corps of Engineers

752
00:42:51,701 --> 00:42:54,300
operates the Cape Cod Canal as part

753
00:42:54,301 --> 00:42:55,760
of a federal recreation area.

754
00:42:55,761 --> 00:43:00,160
There are approximately 3.5 million visitors each year

755
00:43:00,260 --> 00:43:03,060
who enjoy 20 miles of public shoreline

756
00:43:03,160 --> 00:43:07,000
and 1,100 acres of adjoining public parklands.

757
00:43:07,100 --> 00:43:10,100
I think the Cape Cod Canal is a great success story,

758
00:43:10,200 --> 00:43:12,780
not only for navigation purposes,

759
00:43:12,880 --> 00:43:14,880
but because of all the other opportunities

760
00:43:14,980 --> 00:43:16,780
that affords the public.

761
00:43:16,880 --> 00:43:20,080
We are a navigation project, and that's why we're here,

762
00:43:20,180 --> 00:43:21,860
to provide that function.

763
00:43:21,960 --> 00:43:24,880
But we have a recreational component of the canal.

764
00:43:24,881 --> 00:43:27,020
which has developed over the years,

765
00:43:27,120 --> 00:43:30,220
and everything's free here at the canal.

766
00:43:30,320 --> 00:43:32,920
The survival of the canal has been guaranteed

767
00:43:33,020 --> 00:43:37,360
by its many years of irrefutable success.

768
00:43:37,460 --> 00:43:40,000
It's really a marvel.

769
00:43:40,100 --> 00:43:42,160
Of all the years I've lived by the canal,

770
00:43:42,260 --> 00:43:44,300
I have to tell you, every time I see a boat,

771
00:43:44,400 --> 00:43:45,760
I stop and look at it.

772
00:43:45,860 --> 00:43:50,540
It's an unbelievable sight, and it doesn't cost you a penny.

773
00:43:50,640 --> 00:43:52,380
It's the greatest free gift that's

774
00:43:52,381 --> 00:43:56,380
gift that's ever been given to the people around here.

775
00:43:56,480 --> 00:43:58,920
For nearly a century, progress hasn't

776
00:43:59,020 --> 00:44:01,680
diminished the need for a protected sea route along the

777
00:44:01,780 --> 00:44:05,120
eastern seaboard. The Cape Cod Canal has provided

778
00:44:05,220 --> 00:44:08,760
safe passage for millions of vessels and saved countless

779
00:44:08,860 --> 00:44:13,200
lives from the North Atlantic's fury.

